WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.084
LLM API endpoints should require authentication because the service consumes resources and may process sensitive data.

00:00:08.084 --> 00:00:16.100
API keys, service tokens, workload identity, or user-bound credentials can identify which caller is making a request.

00:00:16.100 --> 00:00:18.498
Authentication alone is not enough.

00:00:18.498 --> 00:00:26.719
A valid caller may still need limits on models, tenants, data sources, tools, request size, rate, or allowed operations.

00:00:26.719 --> 00:00:33.296
Authorization should be part of the application design, not an afterthought in the model prompt.

00:00:33.296 --> 00:00:40.078
A user should only be able to submit prompts against data and functions they are allowed to access.

00:00:40.078 --> 00:00:46.792
Tenant separation matters when the same service supports multiple customers, teams, or workspaces.

00:00:46.792 --> 00:00:51.657
Least privilege also applies to applications that call the LLM service.

00:00:51.657 --> 00:00:58.165
A small internal tool should not automatically receive broad access to every model and dataset.

00:00:58.165 --> 00:01:03.166
Exposure control is especially important for private inference endpoints.

00:01:03.166 --> 00:01:12.415
Local-only binding, LAN access, reverse proxies, TLS, network segmentation, and firewall rules all influence who can reach the service.

00:01:12.415 --> 00:01:23.034
A model endpoint that seems harmless on localhost can become a risk if it is accidentally bound to all interfaces or exposed through a misconfigured proxy.

00:01:23.034 --> 00:01:28.035
Avoid unnecessary internet exposure for experimental or private services.

00:01:28.035 --> 00:01:36.668
Operational teams should know where credentials live, how they are rotated, how callers are revoked, and how usage is audited.

00:01:36.668 --> 00:01:43.108
Default keys, shared tokens, and undocumented service accounts create accountability problems.

00:01:43.108 --> 00:01:54.617
Strong API boundaries help prevent unauthorized resource use, accidental data exposure, and uncontrolled access to models that may be expensive or sensitive to operate.
